.TH PERMGUARD 1 "2026-07-31" "permguard 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
permguard \(em report permission\-bit hazards on explicit paths
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B permguard
.RI [ \-\- ]
.I PATH...
.br
.B permguard \-\-help
.br
.B permguard \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B permguard
is a small read\-only ISO C17 command\-line utility that inspects permission
bits on filesystem objects named explicitly by the operator.
The bootstrap slice answers whether any named non\-symbolic entry carries one
or more of four closed mode\-bit hazards: group\-writable, other\-writable,
set\-user\-ID, or set\-group\-ID.
There is no enabling flag, policy file, or process
.B PATH
lookup.
.P
At least one
.I PATH
operand is required.
Before
.BR \-\- ,
a token beginning with
.B \-
is treated as an option;
.B \-\-
ends option processing so operands such as
.B \-artifact
may be inspected.
The only informational forms are sole\-argument
.B \-\-help
and
.BR \-\-version ;
each writes fixed stdout, leaves stderr empty, and exits
.BR 0 .
A bare
.BR \-\- ,
no operands, an unknown option, or an informational option with extra
arguments writes a stable stderr diagnostic, leaves
stdout empty, and exits with status
.BR 2 .
.P
Each operand is inspected once with
.BR lstat (2).
The metadata belongs to the directory entry named by the operand, so a final
symbolic link is never followed and hazards on a link target are never
transferred to the link operand.
A final symbolic link is an operational rejection
.RB ( SYMBOLIC_LINK ,
status
.BR 2 ),
whether its target is safe, hazardous, missing, or inaccessible.
Dangling final symbolic links are likewise rejected as symbolic links, not as
missing\-target errors.
Failure to resolve an intermediate pathname component remains an inspection
error.
.P
The closed hazard taxonomy contains exactly four independent predicates,
evaluated against a successful
.B lstat
result for a non\-symbolic operand with no file\-type heuristics:
.BR GROUP_WRITABLE
.RB ( S_IWGRP ),
.BR OTHER_WRITABLE
.RB ( S_IWOTH ),
.BR SET_USER_ID
.RB ( S_ISUID ),
and
.BR SET_GROUP_ID
.RB ( S_ISGID ).
One operand may emit up to four stdout lines in that fixed rank.
Owner writability, read bits, execute bits, the sticky bit, UID, GID,
filename, extension, and effective access neither create nor suppress a
finding.
Classification uses mode macros from the successful
.B lstat
result only; it does not call
.BR access (2),
open the object, read file contents, or consult ownership trust, ACLs,
capabilities, or mount policy.
.P
Missing paths
.RB ( ENOENT )
report
.BR MISSING ;
inaccessible paths
.RB ( EACCES )
report
.BR INACCESSIBLE ;
other
.B lstat
failures report
.BR INSPECTION_ERROR_N
with the captured decimal
.BR errno .
An empty\-string operand is always
.B INSPECTION_ERROR_N
even when the host reports
.BR ENOENT ,
so it stays distinct from a non\-empty missing path.
After valid CLI parsing, every operand is attempted even after an operational
error; successful findings and error diagnostics are retained, and the final
status is
.B 2
whenever any operational error occurred.
.P
.B Non\-goals.
This bootstrap does not recurse into directories, follow final symbolic links,
canonicalize or deduplicate operands, read or analyze the process
.BR PATH ,
resolve command names, remediate permissions, change credentials, open
inspected file contents, elevate or drop privileges, run as a daemon, use
networking, persist results, or claim that a reported object is exploitable.
Ownership policy, ACL interpretation, Linux capabilities, extended attributes,
mount flags, package inventory, and service inspection are outside this slice.
This manual does not claim that
.B permguard
is released, packaged, or installed, and it does not treat failed Medium\-repair
run
.B ba6dc2fdd199
as a passed delivery.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-help
Write the fixed two\-line usage synopsis to stdout and exit
.BR 0 .
Must be the sole argument.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Write
.B permguard 0.1.0
followed by a newline to stdout and exit
.BR 0 .
Must be the sole argument.
.TP
.B \-\-
End option processing; every following argument is a path operand, including
another literal
.BR \-\- .
.SH OUTPUT
Every hazard is one stdout record:
.P
.RS
.nf
HAZARD<TAB>"ESCAPED_PATH"
.fi
.RE
.P
.B <TAB>
is byte
.BR 0x09 .
.B HAZARD
is one of
.BR GROUP_WRITABLE ,
.BR OTHER_WRITABLE ,
.BR SET_USER_ID ,
or
.BR SET_GROUP_ID .
Inside the quotes, printable ASCII bytes
.B 0x20
through
.B 0x7e
are literal except
.B \&"
and
.BR \e ,
which are written as
.B \e"
and
.BR \e\e ;
every other byte is uppercase
.BR \exHH .
Paths are not canonicalized, normalized, or replaced by a symlink target.
Findings appear in command\-line operand order, then taxonomy rank for each
operand, including duplicate operands.
A completed clean scan writes nothing to stdout.
.P
Diagnostics go only to stderr as fixed ASCII tokens, never
.BR strerror (3)
text.
A path\-specific diagnostic has the form
.BI "permguard: " REASON ": \"" ESCAPED_PATH "\""
using the same escaping as stdout.
A non\-path diagnostic is
.BI "permguard: " REASON .
Stable reasons include
.BR USAGE ,
.BR UNKNOWN_OPTION ,
.BR MISSING ,
.BR INACCESSIBLE ,
.BR SYMBOLIC_LINK ,
.BR INSPECTION_ERROR_N ,
and
.BR STDOUT_WRITE .
Usage and unknown\-option errors are followed by exactly
.BR "usage: permguard [\-\-] PATH..." .
The process ignores
.B SIGPIPE
so a closed stdout pipe becomes a checked stdio failure reporting
.B STDOUT_WRITE
rather than asynchronous signal termination.
No portable total order is promised between stdout and stderr after a caller
merges the streams.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful sole\-argument
.B \-\-help
or
.BR \-\-version ,
or CLI parsing succeeded, every operand was inspected by
.BR lstat ,
no operand was a symbolic link, and none of the four defined hazards was
present.
For an operational scan both stdout and stderr are empty; informational forms
write their fixed stdout and leave stderr empty.
.TP
.B 1
Parsing and all operand inspections succeeded, no operand was a symbolic link,
and at least one defined hazard was found.
Stdout contains all findings in deterministic order; stderr is empty.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, or at least one operational operand error (missing, inaccessible,
final symbolic link, or other
.B lstat
failure), or a checked stdout write/flush failure that reports
.B STDOUT_WRITE
after
.B SIGPIPE
is ignored.
For a valid mixed invocation,
.B permguard
continues inspecting later operands, emits findings for successfully inspected
hazardous operands, emits one stderr diagnostic per failed or symbolic\-link
operand, and returns
.BR 2 .
Operational error precedence therefore overrides a simultaneous hazard that
would otherwise yield status
.BR 1 .
A usage error prevents all inspection and leaves stdout empty.
The current no\-heap bootstrap defines no allocation or product input\-limit
failure class; statuses
.BR 0 ,
.BR 1 ,
and
.B 2
exhaust normal returns.
.SH HOSTILE PATHS AND SPECIAL FILES
.B Hostile path bytes.
Operand bytes are untrusted.
Finding paths and diagnostic paths use the quoted\-byte escaping in
.BR OUTPUT ,
so spaces, quotes, backslashes, tab, newline, ESC, DEL, and other non\-printable
or non\-ASCII bytes cannot forge records or emit raw terminal\-control sequences.
Leading\-dash basenames are inspected only after
.BR \-\- .
.P
.B Symbolic links.
A final symbolic\-link operand is always
.B SYMBOLIC_LINK
with status
.BR 2 ,
including dangling targets and final self\-loops or two\-link loops; the link is
never followed, so target mode bits are never transferred.
A loop in an intermediate pathname component fails
.B lstat
and reports
.B INSPECTION_ERROR_N
with the captured decimal
.B errno
(commonly
.BR ELOOP ),
not
.BR SYMBOLIC_LINK .
.P
.B Permissions.
Classification reads only the named object's successful
.B st_mode
bits.
A mode\-000 final regular file or directory under a searchable parent remains
metadata\-inspectable without opening content; clean mode bits yield status
.B 0
with empty stdout.
A child below a non\-searchable parent may report
.B INACCESSIBLE
when the host returns
.BR EACCES ;
some privileged or unusual filesystems can bypass that denial, so operators must
not treat every blocked directory as a portable oracle.
.B permguard
never changes mode, ownership, or contents across permission transitions it
observes.
.P
.B Special files.
FIFOs and, when present, AF_UNIX socket pathnames are classified with the same
four mode\-bit predicates as regular files and directories.
Neither type is opened, so a FIFO scan must not block on readers or writers.
Character and block device nodes are outside the documented fixture surface
because creating them commonly requires elevated privilege.
File type neither creates nor suppresses a public hazard code.
.P
.B Replacement and concurrency.
Each operand is one point\-in\-time
.B lstat
observation.
An entry replaced immediately before that call is classified from the new
metadata; a replacement after a successful call does not revise the already
captured
.BR "struct\ stat" .
.B permguard
does not detect an undetectable replace\-and\-restore race, does not lock the
path, and does not authorize later use of the named object.
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
.B permguard
is read\-only and requires no special privilege.
It never changes ownership, mode, or contents, never opens inspected objects for
I/O, never executes them, and never follows the final symbolic\-link component of
an operand.
UID and GID from
.B lstat
do not influence the hazard decision.
.P
Operand bytes are untrusted.
Finding paths and diagnostic paths are rendered with the quoted\-byte escaping
described in
.BR OUTPUT ,
so hostile ESC, control, DEL, and non\-ASCII bytes cannot create forged records
or raw terminal\-control sequences on stdout or stderr.
.P
Results are a point\-in\-time metadata observation of
.B st_mode
bits.
Filesystem state may change before or after the single
.B lstat
per operand; a clean result is neither a lock, an authorization decision, nor a
general safety claim.
Focused regression coverage for hostile and unusual filesystem objects lives in
.B tests/test_permguard.py
under
.B docs/permguard\-hostile\-filesystem\-fixtures\-contract.md ;
that contract does not widen the public CLI or taxonomy and does not claim that
.B permguard
is released, packaged, or installed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Inspect a private regular file (clean; exit status
.BR 0 ,
empty stdout):
.P
.RS
.nf
permguard /tmp/private\-file
.fi
.RE
.P
Inspect a leading\-dash operand after ending option processing:
.P
.RS
.nf
permguard \-\- \-artifact
.fi
.RE
.P
Inspect several named paths; group\-writable and other\-writable objects emit
taxonomy lines and the process exits
.B 1
when at least one hazard matches and every operand was successfully inspected.
A symlink named alone is rejected with status
.BR 2 :
.P
.RS
.nf
permguard /tmp/clean /tmp/group\-writable\-file
permguard /tmp/link\-to\-other\-writable\-file
.fi
.RE
